## 5. Database Design

Use PostgreSQL as the system of record.

### 5.1 Core tables

**`companies`**

Stores canonical company identity, company name, sector, identifiers, peer group, and data provenance.

**`financial_periods`**

Stores reported financial values, reporting periods, currencies, publication timestamps, source links, and statement versions.

**`soft_signals`**

Stores auditor resignations, promoter pledging, rating changes, delayed filings, and other relevant governance signals.

**`assessment_runs`**

Stores the date, eligible company count, prediction horizon, alert budget, model version, scoring policy, and execution status.

**`risk_assessments`**

Stores default risk, manipulation screening, indicator values, relationship signals, evidence discrepancies, explanations, ranking, and data-quality warnings.

**`model_versions`**

Stores model artifacts, feature schemas, training-data references, label definitions, validation results, and version identifiers.

**`review_actions`**

Stores analyst decisions, review status, reasons, timestamps, and follow-up actions.

**`import_jobs`**

Stores import status, source details, file hashes, accepted-row counts, rejected-row counts, and validation errors.

**`audit_events`**

Stores important changes to assessments, configurations, reviewer actions, and access-sensitive operations.

### 5.2 Relationship tables

**`entities`**

Represent relevant graph entities:

- Company
- Director
- Promoter
- Auditor or audit firm
- Lender
- Related party
- Transaction counterparty, when supported by available data

Each entity should have a canonical internal identifier and any known source identifiers.

**`entity_relationships`**

Recommended fields:

```text
relationship_id
source_entity_id
target_entity_id
relationship_type
valid_from
valid_to
observed_at
publicly_available_at
source_url
source_name
confidence
verification_status
metadata_json
```

Relationship types must come from an enumerated, documented set.

Store the source and temporal validity of each relationship. Do not assume a relationship was active throughout a company's entire history.

**`network_features`**

Store calculated network indicators for a company and assessment snapshot, including:

```text
company_id
assessment_id
connected_entity_count
connected_distressed_entity_count
related_party_exposure_ratio
counterparty_concentration
network_feature_values_json
relationship_evidence_json
calculated_at
feature_version
```

Optional fields may be null when the underlying data is unavailable. Network features must not be fabricated.

### 5.3 Document evidence tables

**`evidence_documents`**

