## 6. Financial Data and ML Pipeline

### 6.1 UCI benchmark data

Use one of the specified UCI bankruptcy datasets as the initial benchmark.

The coding agent must inspect the selected data before training and document:

- Dataset source and version.
- Target definition and label orientation.
- Available columns and their meanings.
- Class distribution and missing values.
- Duplicates and available company/time identifiers.
- Whether a defensible chronological split is possible.
- Whether the data supports the intended four-quarter prediction horizon.

Do not assume that all datasets contain the same features or a true quarter-level time dimension.

### 6.2 Canonical financial features

Implement documented transformations for applicable financial data:

- Profitability and margin measures.
- Liquidity and working-capital measures.
- Debt, leverage, and interest coverage.
- Operating cash flow.
- Revenue, receivable, debt, and margin changes.
- Relevant historical trends.
- Sector-relative indicators.

Each feature must document its source fields, formula, period requirements, denominator handling, and missing-data behavior.

### 6.3 Financial manipulation indicators

Implement:

- Beneish M-Score.
- Piotroski F-Score.
- Appropriate Altman Z-Score variants.
- Soft-signal indicators for auditor resignation, promoter pledging, rating changes, and delayed filings.
- Additional verified relationship and document-evidence signals.

Calculate each financial indicator only when its required source information is available.

Store the individual component values and the indicator result. Return an explicit unavailable status when required inputs are missing.

Do not describe a financial screening indicator as proof that a company manipulated its accounts.

### 6.4 Default prediction

Build a reproducible model-training pipeline:

1. Audit the chosen UCI dataset.
2. Validate the target and feature mappings.
3. Build a majority-class baseline and regularized logistic regression baseline.
4. Compare a tree-based or boosting model where appropriate.
5. Fit imputers, scalers, feature selection, and other preprocessing only on training data.
6. Tune parameters and probability calibration on validation data.
7. Evaluate once on held-out test data.
8. Save the model artifact, feature schema, label definition, evaluation metrics, and model version.

If source features cannot be mapped semantically to Indian company data, report benchmark-to-India transfer limitations rather than substituting unrelated columns.

Do not claim a calibrated Indian default probability without sufficient supporting evidence.

