## 12. Evaluation and Testing

### 12.1 Required evaluation metrics

Report:

1. Recall at a 5% alert rate.
2. Precision at a 5% alert rate.
3. PR-AUC.
4. Expected cost using the 20:1 false-negative-to-false-positive cost ratio.
5. Lead time before the verified default/distress event.
6. Calibration metrics where sample size permits.
7. Scorable portfolio coverage.
8. False-positive and false-negative counts.
9. Alert volume and review workload.
10. Performance by sector where the sample size supports meaningful interpretation.

Always report class prevalence and metric denominators.

### 12.2 Indian case panel

Build the requested evaluation panel with at least 10 Indian defaulted companies and 30 matched sector peers, across the available three historical pre-event years.

For each record, retain:

```text
company_id
sector
observation_date
publicly_available_at
default_or_distress_event_date
event_definition
source_url
source_name
financial_data_status
relationship_data_status
document_evidence_status
```

Keep external validation cases out of model fitting and threshold selection when reporting independent Indian case-test results.

A panel of 40 companies is small: a 5% alert budget means only two alerts for a 40-company run. Report actual counts and uncertainty. Do not claim statistical proof of production performance from this small panel.

### 12.3 Time-aware evaluation

Use chronological splits where reliable time information exists, and prevent the same company's observations from leaking between splits when evaluating generalization.

Use only features that were available at the corresponding prediction date.

If a UCI dataset lacks row-level dates or company identifiers, document the limitation and do not describe a random split as a time-aware evaluation.

Do not manufacture quarterly history from annual financial observations.

### 12.4 Ablation tests for the four gaps

Demonstrate the contribution of each enhancement using the same evaluation protocol:

| Experiment | Configuration | Question answered |
|---|---|---|
| Baseline | Default model and existing financial indicators | How well does the initial system perform? |
| Relationship-enhanced | Baseline plus verified relationship features | Do network indicators improve prioritization? |
| Evidence-enhanced | Baseline plus verified document discrepancies | Does document reconciliation add useful evidence? |
| Explainability audit | Model explanations and decision provenance enabled | Can analysts reproduce and inspect decisions? |
| Combined | Baseline plus supported relationship and document signals | Does the combined system improve outcomes within the same budget? |

Report each experiment's sample size, feature coverage, alert count, recall at 5%, PR-AUC, expected cost, and lead-time results where available.

Do not claim a component improves detection unless the measured results support that conclusion.

### 12.5 Test requirements

Implement unit and integration tests covering:

- Financial indicator calculations.
- Missing and invalid inputs.
- Point-in-time data selection.
- Prevention of future-data leakage.
- Relationship-path correctness.
- Entity-matching verification.
- Document-claim normalization.
- Reconciliation of reporting periods and units.
- Explanation provenance.
- Deterministic ranking.
- The 5% alert limit.
- The 20:1 error-cost calculation.
- Handling of failed or unavailable model outputs.
- API authentication and validation.

